+# Local Qwen3.5-122B-A10B optimization log
+
+This document records measurements made only in this local checkout. It is not
+intended as an upstream contribution.
+
+> **2026-07-21 environment correction:** work moved to the actual target
+> machine, a Mac mini M1 (T8103) with 16 GB unified memory, macOS 26.5.
+> External USB SSD (X31). Sections below written before this date assumed an
+> M1 Max 32 GB and a 24-26 GiB budget; on this host the practical budget is
+> roughly 10-12 GiB. The `build-m1max-*` directories came from the old host
+> and are not reconfigurable here (stale absolute paths); the active build is
+> `build-m1/`.
+
+## 2026-07-21 first measured runs (M1 mini 16 GB)
+
+Protocol: `llama-cli -c 4096 -n 32 -t 8 -fa on --no-warmup -no-cnv -st`,
+mmap on, monitored with `scripts/m1max-moe/monitor.sh`. Short prompt
+(~40 tokens). Binary: old-host `build-m1max-bench` (works via
+`DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH`), rev `635cdd5fc`.
+
+| Run | Config | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| smoke1 | `-ngl 999 -ncmoe 99` | Metal OOM (`kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory`) during prefill; op-offload staged expert weights to GPU. CLI then fell into interactive mode and busy-spun on EOF (fixed with `-st` + stdin `</dev/null`) |
+| smoke2 | + `--no-op-offload -b 512 -ub 512` | Metal OOM at the post-load memory-check decode |
+| smoke3 | + `GGML_METAL_NO_RESIDENCY=1` | Metal OOM again, ~4 min in (after readahead) |
+| smoke4 | `-ngl 0` (pure CPU) | **First complete run.** PP 1.2 t/s, TG 0.9 t/s, peak RSS 10.9 GiB, wired 1.7 GiB, swap growth 0. Coherent output |
+
+### Root cause of the Metal OOMs (confirmed by smoke2/3)
+
+With `--n-cpu-moe`, expert tensors go to a CPU buffer, but the Metal
+host-pointer buffer still wraps the contiguous file range spanning *all its
+tensors* (`get_mapping_range` in `src/llama-model.cpp`). Non-expert tensors are
+interleaved with experts across the whole file, so the Metal buffer covers
+~37 GiB. Metal must wire the entire referenced buffer at command submission
+(GPU cannot demand-page), which can never fit in 16 GiB - independent of
+residency sets, warmup, or batch size. `GGML_METAL_NO_RESIDENCY=1` does not
+help because wiring at submission is not the residency-set mechanism.
+
+### Local patches (env-gated, default behavior unchanged)
+
+1. `LLAMA_MMAP_NO_PREFETCH` (`src/llama-model.cpp`): skips the whole-file
+   `POSIX_MADV_WILLNEED`. On this host the readahead streams 37.6 GiB through
+   16 GiB of RAM (measured 139 GiB of page-ins in smoke1, i.e. ~3.7x the file:
+   readahead churned its own pages out), evicting everything useful and adding
+   ~1 min to load.
+2. `LLAMA_MMAP_GPU_COPY` (`src/llama-model.cpp`): for non-CPU devices, forces
+   the copy path instead of the host-pointer path, giving Metal a compact
+   buffer holding only its own tensors (~4.2 GiB with all experts on CPU),
+   which is safely wireable. Experts keep the lazy mmap CPU path.
+3. `GGML_MOE_PREFETCH` (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c`): in
+   `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id`, after the router grouping is known,
+   thread 0 issues `posix_madvise(WILLNEED)` for every selected expert slice
+   before compute starts, converting serial 16 KiB demand faults into kernel
+   readahead overlapped with compute.
+4. `LLAMA_QUANT_ONLY_OVERRIDES` (`src/llama-quant.cpp`): `llama-quantize`
+   keeps every tensor not matched by a `--tensor-type` pattern at its current
+   type (same-type tensors are copied bit-exact), enabling expert-only requant
+   passes.
+
+## 2026-07-21 second round: storage + prefetch + threads
+
+Metal OOM verdict: with `LLAMA_MMAP_GPU_COPY` the hybrid runs clean
+(smoke5, wired peak 6.6 GiB), but TG equals CPU-only because generation is
+expert-bound, so Metal offload of attention is not currently the lever.
+Op-offload of experts during prefill (smoke6) makes PP *worse* (0.8 t/s):
+staging copies read the same cold bytes from disk and add GPU traffic.
+MTP speculation (smoke7) also *hurts* (TG 0.5 t/s): verification unions
+near-disjoint expert sets, so it adds I/O for rejected drafts while page
+cache already serves the adjacent-token reuse.
+
+| Step (cumulative) | PP t/s | TG t/s | Note |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| USB SSD, CPU-only, t8 (smoke4/5) | 1.2-1.4 | 0.9 | I/O-latency-bound, CPU 50-130% |
+| + internal NVMe copy (R1/R2) | 1.7-1.8 | 1.0-1.1 | small gain -> latency-bound, not bandwidth |
+| + GGML_MOE_PREFETCH (R3) | 2.7 | 1.2 | CPU jumps to ~600%: faults now overlapped |
+| llama-bench pp256/tg64, t=4 | 5.53 | **2.48** | 4 threads (P-cores only) beats 8 by 33% on TG |
+| llama-bench pp256/tg64, t=8 | 5.62 | 1.87 | E-core contention hurts TG |
+
+llama-bench numbers benefit from warm cache across repetitions; interactive
+cold-start TG is lower. Bottleneck after prefetch: CPU dequant/dot of IQ2_XXS
+(i-quant grid lookups). Next experiment: expert-only requant to Q2_K
+(k-quant, much faster CPU dot), file 38.5 -> 41.4 GB, quality to be
+quantified with llama-perplexity before adoption.
+
+## 2026-07-21 third round: expert requant + active-expert count
+
+Expert-only requant to Q2_K (via the `LLAMA_QUANT_ONLY_OVERRIDES` patch,
+12.7 min on 8 threads): llama-bench t=4 gives tg 2.66 +/- 0.30 vs 2.48 +/- 0.25
+- inside noise, PP slightly worse (5.13 vs 5.53; +2.9 GB file hurts hit rate).
+Conclusion: neither dequant compute nor i-quant lookups dominate; after
+prefetch the limiter is cold expert bytes per token vs page-cache hit rate.
+
+Active-expert-count sweep (`--override-kv qwen35moe.expert_used_count=int:N`),
+llama-cli protocol, expQ2K, internal NVMe, t=4, prefetch on:
+
+| n_expert_used | PP t/s | TG t/s |
+| ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| 8 (default) | 3.0 | 1.8 |
+| 6 | 3.8 | 2.5 |
+| 4 | 5.0 | **4.3** |
+
+Superlinear TG gain at 4 experts: bytes/token halves AND the touched pool
+shrinks, raising cache hit rate. Perplexity comparison across
+{IQ2_M e8, Q2K e8, Q2K e6, Q2K e4} is running; expert-count reduction is a
+quality tradeoff to be decided by the user, not adopted silently.
+
+## Environment
+
+| Item | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| Host | MacBook Pro, Apple M1 Max, 32 GB unified memory |
+| Practical model and runtime budget | 24-26 GB |
+| Model storage | `/Volumes/X31` external USB SSD |
+| Model file | `Qwen3.5-122B-A10B-UD-IQ2_M.gguf` |
+| Downloaded byte count | 40,377,862,848 bytes (37.60 GiB) |
+| llama.cpp revision | `635cdd5fc` |
+| Build | Release, AppleClang 21.0.0.21000101, Metal, Accelerate |
+| Executables | `build-m1max-bench/bin/llama-cli`, `llama-bench`, `llama-perplexity` |
+
+The GGUF exceeds the practical memory budget by 11.6-13.6 GiB before KV cache
+and runtime allocations. `mmap` is therefore a requirement to test, not an
+assumption that the entire model fits.
+
+## Baseline protocol
+
+Every run records wall-clock load time, resident memory, swap use, prompt
+processing throughput, and generation throughput. Generation uses one sequence
+and Q8_0 K/V cache at 8192 tokens. The first comparison is mmap on versus mmap
+off with all layers offloaded to Metal.
+
+| Configuration | Load time | RSS | Swap | PP tok/s | TG tok/s | Result |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| mmap, Metal, Q8 KV, 8k ctx | pending | pending | pending | pending | pending | pending |
+| no-mmap, Metal, Q8 KV, 8k ctx | pending | pending | pending | pending | pending | pending |
+
+## Initial code-path finding
+
+`llama_model_base::load_tensors()` maps the tensor-containing GGUF range into a
+backend buffer through `ggml_backend_dev_buffer_from_host_ptr()`. For Metal on
+Apple Silicon this preserves the file-backed virtual mapping, but it does not
+create a per-expert residency policy. Whether inactive experts remain
+non-resident must be established from measured RSS and page/IO behavior.
+
+`ggml_backend_sched` has a selected-expert copy optimization for offloaded MoE
+weights. The first baseline establishes whether this path is used effectively
+with a Metal host-pointer buffer before any local modification is considered.
+
+## Quantization candidates
+
+| Quantization | Published file size | Relative to 24-26 GiB budget | Decision |
+| --- | ---: | --- | --- |
+| UD-IQ2_M | 40.4 GB | 14.4-16.4 GB over | Baseline target |
+| UD-IQ2_XXS | 40.3 GB | 14.3-16.3 GB over | Too small a memory change to justify quality tradeoff alone |
+| UD-IQ1_M | 38.7 GB | 12.7-14.7 GB over | Fallback only if page residency or quality testing justifies it |
+
+No re-quantization is selected before the baseline. A mixed-precision GGUF
+would require a quality measurement against this baseline and is not assumed to
+be beneficial merely from its nominal size.
+
+## GGUF byte layout (measured from the tensor table, no inference)
+
+The 37.60 GiB file splits cleanly into a small always-resident part and a large
+conditionally-active expert part:
+
+| Group | Size | Share |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| Routed expert weights (`ffn_*_exps`, conditionally active) | 33.363 GiB | 88.7% |
+| Everything else (attn, embed, shared expert, norms, MTP head) | 4.231 GiB | 11.3% |
+
+Quant mix (unsloth UD already applies mixed precision): routed experts are
+IQ2_XXS (18.18 GiB) and IQ3_XXS (13.49 GiB); attention/shared/output are
+Q5_K (2.75 GiB), Q6_K (0.86 GiB), and smaller Q2_K/Q4_K/IQ4_XS pieces.
+
+Architecture metadata: `qwen35moe`, 49 blocks + 1 nextn/MTP layer, n_expert 256,
+n_expert_used 8, n_embd 3072, n_head 32, n_head_kv 2 (GQA), ctx 262144,
+rope_freq_base 1e7. GQA-2 makes the KV cache ~0.16 GiB at 8k with Q8_0, i.e. not
+the memory bottleneck. The bottleneck is the 33.36 GiB expert pool versus a
+~24-26 GiB budget of which ~4.2 GiB is spent on the always-resident part.
+
+## MoE mmap lazy-loading: static code-path analysis (no runs)
+
+Goal of this section: determine, from source only, whether inactive experts can
+stay non-resident, and which knobs control it. Every claim below is a code-path
+fact; residency/RSS/swap numbers still require measurement.
+
+### 1. Weights are mapped without a copy
+
+`llama_model::load_tensors()` takes the mmap+host-ptr path when
+`use_mmap && buffer_from_host_ptr && is_default_buft`
+(`src/llama-model.cpp:1555`) and wraps the file range into a backend buffer via
+`ggml_backend_dev_buffer_from_host_ptr()` (`:1569`). For Metal this reaches
+`ggml_metal_buffer_map()`, which calls `newBufferWithBytesNoCopy` with
+`MTLResourceStorageModeShared` (`ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m:1620`).
+So at buffer-creation time there is no copy and pages remain file-backed. The
+loader confirms the no-copy branch: `ggml_backend_tensor_alloc(buf_mmap, cur,
+data)` with `data = mmap addr + offs` when `cur->data == nullptr`
+(`src/llama-model-loader.cpp:1560`); the `else` branch (`:1571`) is a real copy.
+
+### 2. Three independent mechanisms force full residency by default
+
+Mapping without a copy is necessary but not sufficient. Three separate
+mechanisms each pull the whole model into memory unless disabled:
+
+| Mechanism | Effect | Control | Source |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `POSIX_MADV_WILLNEED` over the whole file | load-time readahead of all 37.6 GiB (advisory, pages evictable) | hardcoded `init_mappings(true)`; needs a patch to gate | `src/llama-model.cpp:1518`, `src/llama-mmap.cpp:462` |
+| Metal residency set `requestResidency` | hard-wires the whole mapped buffer resident; a heartbeat thread re-requests every 5 ms for `keep_alive_s` (default 180 s) after each compute | `GGML_METAL_NO_RESIDENCY=1`; `GGML_METAL_RESIDENCY_KEEP_ALIVE_S` | `ggml-metal-device.m:827-829,1465-1470,566-599`; renewed at `ggml-metal-context.m:451` |
+| Warmup routes to all experts | `n_expert_used` is overridden to `n_expert` (256) during warmup, so the empty run touches every expert and faults in all 33 GiB | `--no-warmup` | `src/llama-graph.cpp:1340`; warmup at `common/common.cpp:1422` |
+
+On macOS 15+ (this host is Darwin 25.5, macOS 26) residency sets are on by
+default, so the default full-Metal-offload run wires the entire 37.6 GiB into a
+32 GiB machine. This is the dominant reason a naive run would swap. The prior
+"mmap on vs off" plan does not capture this third axis; residency and warmup
+must be treated as first-class variables.
+
+### 3. What the compute kernels actually touch
+
+- Generation (batch < 32) dispatches `mul_mv_id` with a grid of
+  `ne20*ne21 = n_expert_used * n_tokens` (`ggml-metal-ops.cpp:2463,2471`). A
+  single-token step launches work for 8 experts only and indexes their rows via
+  the ids tensor. The kernel is genuinely sparse: only the 8 selected experts'
+  pages are read.
+- Prefill (batch >= 32) dispatches `mul_mm_id` with a grid whose third
+  dimension is `ne02 = n_expert = 256` (`:2417`); per-expert threadgroups
+  early-exit when their tokens-per-expert count is zero, but a diverse prompt
+  routes to most experts, so prefill effectively reads the full 33 GiB once.
+  TTFT therefore scales with prompt length/diversity and with SSD bandwidth.
+
+Consequence: the kernel's per-token selectivity only turns into a small
+resident working set if mechanism #2 (residency) is not forcing the whole
+buffer resident. Kernel sparsity and residency wiring are independent; both
+matter.
+
+### 4. Alternative placement: experts in a CPU buffer
+
+`--cpu-moe` / `--n-cpu-moe N` / `-ot ...=CPU` move expert tensors to
+`ggml_backend_cpu_buffer_type()` (`common/arg.cpp:2510-2530`). The CPU device
+also advertises `buffer_from_host_ptr = true`
+(`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.cpp:398`), so with mmap the experts are still
+aliased into a CPU buffer with no copy - but a CPU buffer is never added to a
+Metal residency set, so it is not wired and pages in on demand. Because the CPU
+buffer is host, the scheduler's selected-expert copy path is eligible
+(`ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp:1576-1660`, gated on
+`ggml_backend_buffer_is_host`). With `op_offload` on by default and host-weight
+ops offloaded to GPU only when batch >= `GGML_OP_OFFLOAD_MIN_BATCH` (32,
+`ggml-metal-device.m:848`, `ggml-backend.cpp:919`):
+- generation (batch 1): expert matmuls run on CPU over mmap-backed pages, 8
+  experts per token, lazy paging, no cross-backend copy;
+- prefill (batch >= 32): expert matmuls offload to GPU and only the used experts
+  are copied (grouped runs), not all 256.
+
+### 5. Candidate strategies to benchmark (no strategy chosen yet)
+
+- A. Full Metal offload with `GGML_METAL_NO_RESIDENCY=1` and `--no-warmup`.
+  Experts stay on Metal, unwired, GPU-computed; relies on Metal's automatic
+  per-command residency demand-paging file-backed pages. Fastest compute if the
+  demand-paging assumption holds; that assumption is the main risk and must be
+  measured (RSS/wired/swap), not assumed.
+- B. `--n-cpu-moe N` (or `--cpu-moe`) with `--no-warmup`. Attention/shared stay
+  on Metal (~4.2 GiB, safely wired); experts lazy on CPU. More predictable
+  paging behavior; expert matmul on CPU during generation is the cost. N is a
+  tunable that trades resident GPU footprint against CPU compute share.
+- Both leave the `WILLNEED` load-time readahead in place; gating it is a small,
+  isolated patch candidate once A/B baselines exist.
+
+### 6. What must be measured before any code change
+
+1. Resident vs wired vs swapped bytes for: default; `NO_RESIDENCY+--no-warmup`;
+   `--n-cpu-moe` sweep. Use `footprint`/`vmmap`/`memory_pressure`, not just RSS.
+2. Distinct-expert growth over a generation (does the hot working set saturate
+   under budget, or approach all 256 -> unavoidable thrash?). This decides
+   whether 5 tok/s is reachable at all, or whether re-quant to IQ1_M is required.
+3. External-SSD random-read bandwidth/latency, since page-in of cold experts
+   during generation is bounded by it.
+
+Nothing in sections 1-5 is a completed optimization; they define the variables
+and the escape hatches. No `GGML_*` flag or `--no-warmup` recommendation is
+adopted until the measurements in this section exist.
+
+## Quantization strategy (item 1): a fallback subordinate to residency
+
+Re-quantization is not the primary lever here, and this section explains why,
+with numbers, so the option is on record but correctly ranked.
+
+The file size is not the constraint: the SSD has ~840 GiB free. The constraint
+is the resident working set during generation. Re-quantizing experts helps only
+in two ways: it shrinks the bytes each hot expert occupies while resident, and
+it shrinks the per-token page-in volume when a cold expert is hit. It does
+nothing that keeping experts non-resident (sections 2-5) does not already do
+more cheaply and with zero quality cost.
+
+Measured expert pool: 33.36 GiB, 118.4 B params, current average 2.421 bpw
+(unsloth UD already mixes IQ2_XXS and IQ3_XXS across experts and keeps
+attention/shared/output at Q5_K/Q6_K). Expert budget after the ~4.2 GiB
+always-resident part, KV, and compute buffers is roughly 17-20 GiB. So the hot
+fraction of experts that must stay resident, at each quantization, to fit:
+
+| Option | Est. expert pool | Max hot-fraction that fits ~17-20 GiB | Source needed | Quality risk |
+| --- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |
+| Current UD-IQ2_M (measured) | 33.36 GiB | ~51-60% | already local | baseline |
+| All experts IQ2_XXS (2.06 bpw) | ~28.4 GiB | ~60-70% | requant needs BF16/Q8 src + imatrix | low-moderate |
+| unsloth UD-IQ1_M (pre-quant) | ~31.8 GiB | ~53-63% | download (~36 GiB); marginal gain | moderate (UD keeps key tensors high) |
+| All experts IQ1_M (1.75 bpw) | ~24.1 GiB | ~71-83% | requant needs BF16/Q8 src + imatrix | high |
+| All experts IQ1_S (1.56 bpw) | ~21.5 GiB | ~79-93% | requant needs BF16/Q8 src + imatrix | severe |
+
+Two hard constraints on the re-quant path:
+
+1. Clean sub-IQ2 quantization needs a higher-precision source (BF16 or a high-bit
+   GGUF) plus an importance matrix (`llama-quantize --imatrix --tensor-type
+   ffn_*_exps=IQ1_M`). Neither the BF16 source nor an imatrix is present locally;
+   the source is a large download. Requantizing from the existing IQ2_M with
+   `--allow-requantize` is lossy-on-lossy and its quality must not be assumed
+   acceptable - it would need a `llama-perplexity` comparison against the current
+   file before adoption.
+2. Even all-experts IQ1_S (severe quality cost) does not guarantee a 100%-hot
+   working set fits under 20 GiB. So re-quant extends the allowable hot fraction
+   but does not remove the dependence on residency behavior.
+
+Decision rule (no change adopted yet): run the section-6 measurements first. If
+Strategy A or B keeps the hot working set inside budget at the current quant,
+re-quant is unnecessary (preferred, zero quality cost). Only if the measured hot
+fraction lands moderately over budget does a targeted expert re-quant (IQ2_XXS,
+then IQ1_M) become worth a perplexity-measured quality tradeoff, presented as an
+explicit choice rather than decided here.
+
