+20 tok/s = 50 ms/token. At ~2.5 GB/s effective read that is a ~100 MB cold-read
+budget per token, an 8-12x reduction from the measured 0.7-1.0 GiB/token. No
+prefetch/pipelining can close that gap; the expert working set must become (near)
+RAM-resident. Once resident, the CPU decode ceiling is RAM-bandwidth-bound:
+~3.4 GB active weights/token at IQ2_M vs ~40-60 GB/s => 12-17 tok/s; sub-2-bit
+experts and/or expert_used_count=6 push the ceiling to ~20-25 tok/s.
+
+### New local patches (env-gated, additive to the 2026-07-21 set)
+
+- `GGML_MOE_PREFETCH_AHEAD=N` (ggml-cpu.c): at each mul_mat_id, after the
+  routing-group scan, additionally advise the expert slices of the next N
+  mul_mat_id ops in graph order. Ops sharing the current routing tensor
+  (gate/up/down of the same layer, matched by ids tensor name) get exact
+  prefetch; other layers get a prediction = the experts they used for the
+  previous token. Only active for generation-sized batches (n_tokens <= 4) and
+  requires GGML_MOE_PREFETCH=1. Literature anchors: next-layer reuse/prediction
+  recall 55-96% (Mixtral-offloading arXiv:2312.17238, HOBBIT arXiv:2411.01433).
+- `GGML_MOE_STATS=<path>` (ggml-cpu.c): accumulates routed-row counts per
+  (exps tensor, expert) across the run, dumped as CSV at exit. Feeds the
+  pruning and pinning tools below.
+
+### New offline tools (scripts/m1max-moe/)
+
+- `prune_experts.py`: bit-exact expert pruning of a GGUF (slices the expert
+  dim of ffn_*_exps + router rows, rewrites {arch}.expert_count; no dequant).
+  Ranking from a GGML_MOE_STATS CSV or an explicit keep-list. Verified dry-run
+  against the local IQ2_M file: keep=128 => 37.59 -> 20.84 GiB.
+- `expert_ranges.py` + `pin_ranges.c`: compute hottest expert byte ranges from
+  a stats CSV and mlock them from a sidecar process (file-backed pages are
+  shared with llama.cpp's own mapping), protecting hot experts from eviction.
+
+### Research conclusions driving the 20 tok/s plan (2026-07-22 survey)
+
+- Expert pruning is the main structural lever: REAP (arXiv:2510.13999) reports
+  ~97% retention at 50% prune on Qwen3-family (with a contradicting AIMER
+  report at 50% - start at 25-35%). A community REAP of exactly our base model
+  exists: OpenMOSE/Qwen3.5-REAP-97B-A10B-GGUF (256->200 experts, IQ2_M 31.7 GB)
+  - download started to /Volumes/X31/optillama/models/.
+- MoE-Infinity (arXiv:2401.14361) measures <5% of experts repeatedly active
+  within a single request for 100+-expert models => per-sequence hot set may fit
+  the ~10 GiB cache even before pruning; the stats patch will verify this on
+  our routing.
+- Stacking estimate: lookahead prefetch (x1.2-1.35) + expert_used_count=6
+  (x1.3, +4.8% PPL measured) + prune-to-resident (structural) => 12-22 tok/s.
+- llama-server target added to the build (untested against the model).
+
+All numbers above are literature-derived estimates, not local measurements;
+every step lands behind a perplexity + tok/s measurement gate, and quality
+tradeoffs (pruning depth, expert count, sub-2-bit) remain user decisions.
